\title{Ketogenic Diet for Drug-Resistant Epilepsy in Children:
A Randomized Controlled Trial}
\author{The KETOPED Collaborative Group}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
\input{abstract}
\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}
Drug-resistant epilepsy, defined as seizures that persist despite
adequate trials of two antiseizure medications, carries a high risk
of injury and impaired neurodevelopment in children.
Dietary therapy has been proposed as an adjunctive treatment, but
earlier studies yielded conflicting results.
\section{Methods}
We conducted a multicenter, randomized, controlled trial at
seventeen centers in four countries.
Eligible patients were randomly assigned in a one-to-one ratio to
initiate a ketogenic diet or to continue optimized medical therapy.
The primary efficacy endpoint was the proportion of patients with at
least 50 percent reduction from baseline in seizure frequency at six
months.
